    "topicId": "04_Transformations_and_Functions_of_a_Random_Variable",
    "topicTitle": "04 \u00b7 Transformations of Random Variables",
    "cardNumber": "01",
    "category": "CDF METHOD",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "The CDF Method for Transformations Y = g(X)",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-30401\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-30401)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-30401)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">x</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">y = g(x)</text><path d=\"M40.0,52.0 L43.2,55.8 L46.4,59.4 L49.6,62.8 L52.8,66.1 L56.0,69.3 L59.2,72.3 L62.4,75.1 L65.6,77.8 L68.8,80.3 L72.0,82.7 L75.2,84.9 L78.4,87.0 L81.6,88.9 L84.8,90.7 L88.0,92.3 L91.2,93.8 L94.4,95.1 L97.6,96.2 L100.8,97.2 L104.0,98.1 L107.2,98.8 L110.4,99.3 L113.6,99.7 L116.8,99.9 L120.0,100.0 L123.2,99.9 L126.4,99.7 L129.6,99.3 L132.8,98.8 L136.0,98.1 L139.2,97.2 L142.4,96.2 L145.6,95.1 L148.8,93.8 L152.0,92.3 L155.2,90.7 L158.4,88.9 L161.6,87.0 L164.8,84.9 L168.0,82.7 L171.2,80.3 L174.4,77.8 L177.6,75.1 L180.8,72.3 L184.0,69.3 L187.2,66.1 L190.4,62.8 L193.6,59.4 L196.8,55.8 L200.0,52.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><line x1=\"24.0\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"226.0\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"222\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">y</text><rect x=\"56.754446796632415\" y=\"108\" width=\"126.49110640673517\" height=\"4\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#d9dee7\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"56.8\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"56.8\" y2=\"108.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><line x1=\"183.2\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"183.2\" y2=\"108.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">{x : g(x) \u2264 y}</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">FY(y) = \u2119(g(X) \u2264 y)</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">= \u222b fX(x) dx over the shaded x</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">ask the question about X, then differentiate in y</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The curve y = g(x) and a level y. The shaded stretch of the x-axis is {x : g(x) \u2264 y}, and the chance of that stretch under f_X is F_Y(y) = \u2119(g(X) \u2264 y).",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Universal 2-Step Protocol:</strong> Step 1: express the CDF of $Y$ directly in terms of $X$: $F_Y(y) = \\mathbb{P}(Y \\le y) = \\mathbb{P}(g(X) \\le y) = \\int_{\\{x : g(x) \\le y\\}} f_X(x)\\,dx$.",
      "<strong>Differentiation Step:</strong> Step 2: differentiate $F_Y(y)$ with respect to $y$ (via Fundamental Theorem of Calculus or Leibniz rule) to find density $f_Y(y) = F_Y'(y)$.",
      "<strong>Universal Validity:</strong> Works unconditionally for any measurable $g$ (monotonic, non-monotonic, multi-to-one, piecewise) without requiring invertibility."
    ],
    "math": "$$F_Y(y) = \\mathbb{P}(g(X) \\le y) = \\int_{\\{x: g(x) \\le y\\}} f_X(x)\\,dx, \\qquad f_Y(y) = \\frac{d}{dy} F_Y(y)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>From ride time to something else.</strong> Whatever you compute from the ride (speed, fare, minutes late), ask which ride times make it at most $y$, add up their chance, and you have the CDF of the new quantity.",
    "examTrap": "Differentiating without determining the valid support of Y! You must track y \u2208 g(supp(X)); omitting indicator functions 1_{y \u2208 support} leads to invalid densities with infinite integrals.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>CDF method.</b> Let $X$ be a random variable and $g:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ measurable, and put $Y=g(X)$. Then $F_Y(y)=\\mathbb P(g(X)\\le y)=\\mathbb P\\bigl(X\\in\\{x:g(x)\\le y\\}\\bigr)$, which equals $\\int_{\\{x:g(x)\\le y\\}}f_X(x)\\,dx$ when $X$ has density $f_X$. If $F_Y$ is differentiable, $f_Y(y)=\\dfrac{d}{dy}F_Y(y)$ on the support of $Y$ (Ross 5.7; Grimmett 4.7).",
    "hint": "How do you find the law of a new quantity built from the ride time, without any formula to memorise?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\{x:g(x)\\le y\\}$ = the rides that give a small enough $Y$.</strong> Turn the question about $Y$ into a question about which ride times $x$ qualify.",
      "<strong>$\\int_{\\{x:g(x)\\le y\\}}f_X(x)\\,dx$ = add up their chance.</strong> The shaded part of the ride-time axis carries its area under $f_X$.",
      "<strong>$f_Y=\\dfrac{d}{dy}F_Y$ = differentiate for the density.</strong> Do it only on the values $y$ that $Y$ can really take."
    ],
