    "id": 30406,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "04_Transformations_and_Functions_of_a_Random_Variable",
    "topicTitle": "04 \u00b7 Transformations of Random Variables",
    "cardNumber": "06",
    "category": "WORKED BENCHMARK",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "Worked Benchmark: Tangent Transform & The Cauchy Law",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-30406\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"222\" y=\"36\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">wall: x</text><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"112\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">you (distance 1)</text><line x1=\"120.0\" y1=\"112.0\" x2=\"168.0\" y2=\"40.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-30406)\"/><text x=\"144\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u0398</text><circle cx=\"168\" cy=\"40\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"168\" y=\"32\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X = tan \u0398</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">ray at a random angle \u0398</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u0398 ~ Unif(\u2212\u03c0/2, \u03c0/2)</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">fX(x) = 1/(\u03c0(1 + x\u00b2))</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">heavy tails: \ud835\udd3c[X] does not exist</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">a GPS ping at a random angle lands tan \u0398 along the wall</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "From you at distance 1, a ray at random angle \u0398 hits the wall at X = tan \u0398. The resulting density 1/(\u03c0(1+x\u00b2)) has heavy tails, so \ud835\udd3c[X] does not exist.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Projection Setup:</strong> Angle $\\Theta \\sim \\text{Uniform}(-\\pi/2, \\pi/2)$ with density $f_\\Theta(\\theta) = 1/\\pi$. Define shoreline coordinate $X = \\tan(\\Theta)$.",
      "<strong>Jacobian Step:</strong> Inverse is $\\theta = \\arctan(x)$, with derivative $\\left|\\frac{d\\theta}{dx}\\right| = \\frac{1}{1 + x^2}$.",
      "<strong>Standard Cauchy PDF:</strong> $f_X(x) = f_\\Theta(\\arctan x) \\left|\\frac{d\\theta}{dx}\\right| = \\frac{1}{\\pi (1 + x^2)}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$. The integral $\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty |x| f_X(x)dx = \\infty$, so $\\mathbb{E}[X]$ does not exist!"
    ],
    "math": "$$X = \\tan\\Theta, \\; \\Theta \\sim \\text{Unif}\\left(-\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\implies f_X(x) = \\frac{1}{\\pi (1 + x^2)}, \\quad \\mathbb{E}[X] \\text{ does not exist!}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>A GPS ping at a random angle.</strong> A ping that points anywhere in the half-circle ahead lands somewhere along the street wall. Most land near you, but a few land absurdly far away, and these far pings ruin any average.",
    "examTrap": "Claiming E[X] = 0 by symmetry! For Cauchy, the positive tail integral is +\u221e and the negative tail is -\u221e. The expression \u221e - \u221e is undefined, so the expected value does not exist.",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Cauchy law from a random angle.</b> If $\\Theta\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ and $X=\\tan\\Theta$, then $F_X(x)=\\mathbb P(\\Theta\\le\\arctan x)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac1\\pi\\arctan x$ and $f_X(x)=\\dfrac1{\\pi(1+x^2)}$, the standard Cauchy density. Its tails are so heavy that $\\int|x|f_X(x)\\,dx=\\infty$, so $\\mathbb E[X]$ does not exist (Ross 5.7; Grimmett 4.4).",
    "hint": "If a ray leaves you at a uniformly random angle, where does it hit a wall, and why is its average undefined?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\Theta\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ = a random direction.</strong> Every direction facing the wall is equally likely.",
      "<strong>$X=\\tan\\Theta$ = where the ray meets the wall.</strong> With the wall at distance 1, the hit point is $\\tan\\Theta$ along it; near the sideways angles it shoots off to $\\pm\\infty$.",
      "<strong>$f_X(x)=\\frac{1}{\\pi(1+x^2)}$ = heavy tails.</strong> The tails fall only like $1/x^2$, so the positive and negative halves of $\\int xf_X$ are $+\\infty$ and $-\\infty$, and the mean is undefined."
    ],
