    "id": 30801,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "08_Multiple_Random_Variables_and_Order_Statistics",
    "topicTitle": "08 \u00b7 Multiple RVs & Order Statistics",
    "cardNumber": "01",
    "category": "EXTREME VALUES",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "Order Statistics & Extremes: Sample Minimum and Maximum",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-30801\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-30801)\"/><text x=\"222\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">time</text><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2081</text><circle cx=\"96\" cy=\"60\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2082</text><circle cx=\"128\" cy=\"60\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"128\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2083</text><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"60\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2084</text><circle cx=\"196\" cy=\"60\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"196\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2085</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u208d\u2081\u208e first</text><text x=\"196\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u208d\u2099\u208e last</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">max \u2264 x: all n arrive by x:  [F(x)]\u207f</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"114\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">min > x: none yet:  1 \u2212 [1 \u2212 F(x)]\u207f</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">n buses on different routes, each arrival time X\u1d62</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"36\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">f of the first = n[1 \u2212 F(x)]\u207f\u207b\u00b9 f(x)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">first = survivors multiply; last = arrivals multiply</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "n arrival times X\u1d62 on a time line, with the first X\u208d\u2081\u208e and the last X\u208d\u2099\u208e marked. The last is at most x if all arrive, [F(x)]\u207f; the first exceeds x if none have, [1 \u2212 F(x)]\u207f.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Ascending Sort:</strong> For i.i.d. $X_1, \\dots, X_n \\sim F$, sorting in ascending order yields order statistics $X_{(1)} \\le X_{(2)} \\le \\dots \\le X_{(n)}$.",
      "<strong>Sample Maximum:</strong> $X_{(n)} \\le x \\iff$ every $X_i \\le x$. By independence, $F_{X_{(n)}}(x) = [F(x)]^n$, so $f_{X_{(n)}}(x) = n [F(x)]^{n-1} f(x)$.",
      "<strong>Sample Minimum:</strong> $X_{(1)} > x \\iff$ every $X_i > x$. Thus $\\mathbb{P}(X_{(1)} > x) = [1 - F(x)]^n$, giving $F_{X_{(1)}}(x) = 1 - [1 - F(x)]^n$."
    ],
    "math": "$$F_{X_{(n)}}(x) = [F(x)]^n, \\qquad F_{X_{(1)}}(x) = 1 - [1 - F(x)]^n, \\qquad f_{X_{(1)}}(x) = n[1-F(x)]^{n-1} f(x)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Which bus gets you there first?</strong> With several routes to campus, the first arrival is the shortest of several waits: it is short unless every route is slow. The last arrival is long unless every route is quick.",
    "examTrap": "Differentiating 1 - [1 - F(x)]^n and losing the negative sign from the inner derivative! -n[1 - F(x)]^{n-1}(-f(x)) = +n[1 - F(x)]^{n-1}f(x).",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Distribution of the extremes.</b> Let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be i.i.d. with CDF $F$ and density $f$, and let $X_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le X_{(n)}$ be their order statistics. Then $F_{X_{(n)}}(x)=[F(x)]^n$ and $F_{X_{(1)}}(x)=1-[1-F(x)]^n$, so $f_{X_{(n)}}(x)=n[F(x)]^{n-1}f(x)$ and $f_{X_{(1)}}(x)=n[1-F(x)]^{n-1}f(x)$ (Ross 6.6; Grimmett 4.14).",
    "hint": "With n independent buses on different routes, how do you get the law of the first one to arrive and of the last?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$[F(x)]^n$ = all $n$ have arrived by $x$.</strong> The latest arrival is at most $x$ exactly when every bus is at most $x$; independent chances multiply.",
      "<strong>$1-[1-F(x)]^n$ = at least one has arrived by $x$.</strong> The earliest arrival is greater than $x$ only if all $n$ are greater: $[1-F(x)]^n$, so subtract it from 1.",
      "<strong>$n[1-F(x)]^{n-1}f(x)$ = the first one's density.</strong> Differentiate; the inner derivative of $1-F$ is $-f$, which cancels the leading minus sign."
    ],
