    "cardNumber": "04",
    "category": "VARIANCE DECOMPOSITION",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-purple",
    "title": "Law of Total Variance (Eve\u2019s Law): Noise vs Signal",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31204\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"30\" y=\"56\" width=\"180\" height=\"24\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"56\" width=\"120\" height=\"24\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#d9dee7\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"56\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Var(\ud835\udd3c[Y | X]) signal</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c[Var(Y | X)] noise</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Var(Y)</text><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Var(Y) = \ud835\udd3c[Var(Y | X)] + Var(\ud835\udd3c[Y | X])</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">MMSE error = the noise part: what knowing X cannot remove</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "One bar for Var(Y), split into the signal Var(\ud835\udd3c[Y | X]) and the noise \ud835\udd3c[Var(Y | X)]. The noise segment is the minimum mean squared error.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Formal Decomposition:</strong> $\\operatorname{Var}(Y) = \\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{Var}(Y \\mid X)] + \\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid X])$.",
      "<strong>Signal vs Noise:</strong> $\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid X])$ is the variance explained by observation $X$ (signal), while $\\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{Var}(Y \\mid X)]$ is the residual unexplained dispersion (noise).",
      "<strong>MMSE Error Formula:</strong> The minimum mean squared estimation error is precisely the expected conditional variance: $\\mathbb{E}[(Y - \\hat{Y}_{\\text{MMSE}})^2] = \\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{Var}(Y \\mid X)] \\le \\operatorname{Var}(Y)$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\operatorname{Var}(Y) = \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\operatorname{Var}(Y \\mid X)\\right] + \\operatorname{Var}\\left(\\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid X]\\right), \\qquad \\text{MMSE Error} = \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\operatorname{Var}(Y \\mid X)\\right]$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Explained and unexplained.</strong> Part of the variation in rides is predictable from the ETA and part is not. Together they make up the total variation, and the unpredictable part is your unavoidable error.",
    "examTrap": "Missing one of the two terms in Eve's law! Forgetting Var(E[Y|X]) or E[Var(Y|X)] creates severe underestimates of total population volatility.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Law of total variance.</b> If $\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\lt \\infty$, $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid X)]+\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X])$. Hence the minimum mean squared error of estimating $Y$ from $X$ is $\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid X)]$ (Ross 7.5; Grimmett 3.7).",
    "hint": "How does the total spread of the ride split into what the signal explains and what stays noise?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X])$ = the signal.</strong> How much the best guess itself moves from morning to morning.",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid X)]$ = the noise.</strong> The average leftover spread inside each slice of $X$.",
      "<strong>$=\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$ = they add.</strong> The noise part is exactly the minimum mean squared error."
    ],
