    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "Fundamental Theorem: All Linear Projections are Gaussian",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31302\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><ellipse cx=\"76\" cy=\"56\" rx=\"46\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\" transform=\"rotate(-30 76 56)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"104\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"134\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">t</text><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M140.0,111.3 L142.2,110.9 L144.3,110.5 L146.4,109.9 L148.6,109.1 L150.8,108.1 L152.9,106.8 L155.1,105.3 L157.2,103.4 L159.3,101.3 L161.5,98.9 L163.7,96.3 L165.8,93.4 L167.9,90.5 L170.1,87.5 L172.2,84.7 L174.4,82.1 L176.6,79.8 L178.7,78.0 L180.8,76.7 L183.0,76.1 L185.2,76.1 L187.3,76.8 L189.4,78.1 L191.6,79.9 L193.8,82.2 L195.9,84.9 L198.1,87.7 L200.2,90.7 L202.3,93.6 L204.5,96.5 L206.7,99.1 L208.8,101.5 L210.9,103.6 L213.1,105.4 L215.2,106.9 L217.4,108.1 L219.6,109.1 L221.7,109.9 L223.8,110.5 L226.0,110.9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"184\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">t\u1d40X</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udca9(t\u1d40\u03bc, t\u1d40\u03a3t)</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">X ~ \ud835\udca9(\u03bc, \u03a3) \u21d4 t\u1d40X normal for all t</text><text x=\"109.25\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">any weighted mix of the legs is a bell, with mean and variance from \u03bc and \u03a3</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A cloud X and a direction t. The projection t\u1d40X onto t is a bell with mean t\u1d40\u03bc and variance t\u1d40\u03a3t, and this holds for every direction t.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Fundamental Characterization:</strong> $\\mathbf{X}$ is jointly Gaussian if and only if every linear combination $a_1 X_1 + \\dots + a_n X_n = \\mathbf{t}^\\top \\mathbf{X}$ is a 1D Gaussian for all $\\mathbf{t} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$.",
      "<strong>Linear Transformation Invariance:</strong> If $\\mathbf{X} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}, \\Sigma)$ and $Y = A \\mathbf{X} + b$, then $Y$ is strictly Gaussian: $Y \\sim \\mathcal{N}(A\\boldsymbol{\\mu} + b, A \\Sigma A^\\top)$.",
      "<strong>Complete Parametrization:</strong> A joint Gaussian law is 100% specified by its mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$; all higher cumulants are identically zero."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mathbf{X} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}, \\Sigma) \\iff \\mathbf{t}^\\top \\mathbf{X} \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\left(\\mathbf{t}^\\top \\boldsymbol{\\mu}, \\; \\mathbf{t}^\\top \\Sigma \\mathbf{t}\\right) \\quad \\forall \\mathbf{t} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Every mix is a bell.</strong> Whatever weighted combination you take of the wait, the ride and the walk, say 1\u00d7 walk + 2\u00d7 wait, the result has a bell-curve law with a mean and variance you can read off.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming marginally Gaussian variables are jointly Gaussian! Having X ~ N(0, 1) and Y ~ N(0, 1) does NOT imply (X, Y) is jointly Gaussian (e.g. Y = SX with sign flip S).",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Linear projections.</b> $X\\sim\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\Sigma)$ in $\\mathbb R^n$ if and only if for every $t\\in\\mathbb R^n$, $t^\\top X\\sim\\mathcal N(t^\\top\\mu,\\,t^\\top\\Sigma t)$. Consequently, $AX+b\\sim\\mathcal N(A\\mu+b,A\\Sigma A^\\top)$ (Ross 7.8; Grimmett 4.9).",
    "hint": "What law does any weighted total of jointly Gaussian legs follow?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$t^\\top X$ = a weighted total of the legs.</strong> For instance walk plus twice the wait: look at the cloud along direction $t$.",
      "<strong>$t^\\top\\mu$ = the weighted mean.</strong> Means combine linearly, as always.",
      "<strong>$t^\\top\\Sigma t$ = the weighted variance.</strong> Spread along $t$ comes from the covariance table; the shape is still a bell for every $t$."
    ],
