    "topicId": "13_Jointly_Gaussian_Random_Variables",
    "topicTitle": "13 \u00b7 Jointly Gaussian Random Variables",
    "cardNumber": "05",
    "category": "GAUSSIAN ALGEBRA",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "Direct Matrix Inversion: Reading Moments from Quadratic Forms",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31305\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"20\" y=\"36\" width=\"70\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"55\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">[ a  b ]</text><text x=\"55\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">[ b  c ]</text><text x=\"55\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2212\u00bd z\u1d40Pz in the exponent</text><line x1=\"96.0\" y1=\"56.0\" x2=\"134.0\" y2=\"56.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31305)\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">invert</text><rect x=\"140\" y=\"36\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#d9dee7\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">1/(ac\u2212b\u00b2) [ c  \u2212b ]</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">[ \u2212b   a ]</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03a3 = P\u207b\u00b9</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">the matrix in the exponent is the INVERSE covariance</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"114\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">read variances from \u03a3 = P\u207b\u00b9, not from P</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">e.g. Var(X) = c/(ac \u2212 b\u00b2)</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The matrix P in the exponent, inverted: \u03a3 = P\u207b\u00b9 = (1/(ac \u2212 b\u00b2)) [c, \u2212b; \u2212b, a]. Variances come from \u03a3, such as Var(X) = c/(ac \u2212 b\u00b2), not from P directly.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Exponent Identification:</strong> The exponent is $-\\frac{1}{2} (\\mathbf{x} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu})^\\top \\Sigma^{-1} (\\mathbf{x} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu})$. The matrix of quadratic coefficients IS $\\Sigma^{-1}$!",
      "<strong>2D Analytical Inversion:</strong> If $\\Sigma^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & c \\end{bmatrix}$, then $\\Sigma = \\frac{1}{ac - b^2}\\begin{bmatrix} c & -b \\\\ -b & a \\end{bmatrix}$.",
      "<strong>Direct Moment Extraction:</strong> $\\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\frac{c}{ac - b^2}$, $\\operatorname{Var}(Y) = \\frac{a}{ac - b^2}$, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = \\frac{-b}{ac - b^2}$. Zero integration needed!"
    ],
    "math": "$$-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}^\\top \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\implies \\Sigma = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ac - b^2}\\begin{bmatrix}c & -b \\\\ -b & a\\end{bmatrix}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Recipe from the formula.</strong> Someone hands you the exponent of the joint density of the phone error and the ride. Invert its matrix and you get the covariances, so you know how the two move together.",
    "examTrap": "Forgetting the factor of 2 in cross-term 2b xy! The off-diagonal element of \u03a3\u207b\u00b9 is b, NOT 2b. The matrix must be symmetric.",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Reading moments from the exponent.</b> If $f(x,y)\\propto\\exp\\!\\bigl(-\\tfrac12[x\\ y]\\,P\\,[x\\ y]^\\top\\bigr)$ with $P=\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\b&c\\end{bmatrix}\\succ0$, then $(X,Y)$ is centred Gaussian with $\\Sigma=P^{-1}=\\dfrac1{ac-b^2}\\begin{bmatrix}c&-b\\\\-b&a\\end{bmatrix}$, so $\\operatorname{Var}X=\\dfrac{c}{ac-b^2}$ (Grimmett 4.9).",
    "hint": "Given only the quadratic form in the exponent, how do you read off variances and covariance?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$-\\tfrac12z^\\top Pz$ = the exponent.</strong> The matrix in the exponent is the inverse covariance $P=\\Sigma^{-1}$ (the precision matrix).",
      "<strong>$\\Sigma=P^{-1}$ = invert it.</strong> For a $2\\times2$ matrix, swap the diagonal, negate the off-diagonal, and divide by the determinant $ac-b^2$.",
      "<strong>$\\operatorname{Var}X=\\frac{c}{ac-b^2}$ = not $1/a$.</strong> The variance is read from $\\Sigma$, never from the diagonal of $P$; $\\operatorname{Cov}=\\frac{-b}{ac-b^2}$."
    ],
