    "drawingCaption": "Histograms of X\u2099 sharpening around the constant c. A law that piles up at a point is the same as convergence in probability; thinning the days then gives a.s. convergence.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Constant Limit Bridge:</strong> If $X_n \\xrightarrow{d} c$ where $c$ is a deterministic CONSTANT, then $X_n \\xrightarrow{P} c$ (distribution convergence promotes to probability convergence).",
      "<strong>Dominated Convergence Bridge:</strong> If $X_n \\xrightarrow{P} X$ and $|X_n| \\le Y$ with $\\mathbb{E}[Y^p] \\lt  \\infty$, then $X_n \\xrightarrow{L^p} X$ (Lebesgue Dominated Convergence Theorem).",
      "<strong>Subsequential Bridge:</strong> If $X_n \\xrightarrow{P} X$, there exists a SUBSEQUENCE $\\{n_k\\}$ such that $X_{n_k} \\xrightarrow{a.s.} X$."
    ],
    "math": "$$X_n \\xrightarrow{d} c \\iff X_n \\xrightarrow{P} c \\quad (c \\text{ constant}); \\qquad X_n \\xrightarrow{P} X \\implies \\exists \\{n_k\\} \\text{ s.t. } X_{n_k} \\xrightarrow{a.s.} X$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>When the end result is a number.</strong> If the histogram of your average commute piles up at one number, then on a typical day you are close to it; and a thinned-out set of days will settle in the strong sense.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming X_n \u2192^d X implies X_n \u2192^P X for non-constant X! E.g. let X ~ N(0, 1) and X_n = -X. Then X_n has the same normal distribution, but X_n - X = -2X never converges to 0.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Partial converses.</b> (i) If $c$ is a constant, $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}c$ iff $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}c$. (ii) If $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}X$, there is a subsequence $n_k$ with $X_{n_k}\\xrightarrow{a.s.}X$. (iii) If $X_n\\xrightarrow{P}X$ and $|X_n|\\le Y$ with $\\mathbb E[Y]\\lt \\infty$, then $X_n\\xrightarrow{L^1}X$ (Grimmett 7.3; Ross 8.2).",
    "hint": "When do the weaker modes imply the stronger ones after all?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$X_n\\xrightarrow{d}c\\iff X_n\\xrightarrow{P}c$ = a constant limit.</strong> If the histogram piles up at a single value $c$, then $X_n$ is within $\\varepsilon$ of $c$ with chance tending to 1.",
      "<strong>$\\exists\\,n_k$ with a.s. convergence = pick a thin enough subsequence.</strong> If $X_n\\to X$ in probability, pick days where the miss chance is summable; Borel\u2013Cantelli then finishes the job.",
      "<strong>Domination = no mass escapes.</strong> A fixed integrable cap $Y$ keeps the average error from hiding in rare huge values."
    ],
