    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31505\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31505)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31505)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">x</text><path d=\"M30.0,99.7 L33.8,99.3 L37.6,98.6 L41.4,97.5 L45.2,95.6 L49.0,92.7 L52.8,88.6 L56.6,83.3 L60.4,76.8 L64.2,69.7 L68.0,62.5 L71.8,56.3 L75.6,51.9 L79.4,50.0 L83.2,51.0 L87.0,54.7 L90.8,60.4 L94.6,67.4 L98.4,74.6 L102.2,81.3 L106.0,87.1 L109.8,91.5 L113.6,94.8 L117.4,97.0 L121.2,98.3 L125.0,99.1 L128.8,99.6 L132.6,99.8 L136.4,99.9 L140.2,100.0 L144.0,100.0 L147.8,100.0 L151.6,100.0 L155.4,100.0 L159.2,100.0 L163.0,100.0 L166.8,100.0 L170.6,100.0 L174.4,100.0 L178.2,100.0 L182.0,100.0 L185.8,100.0 L189.6,100.0 L193.4,100.0 L197.2,100.0 L201.0,100.0 L204.8,100.0 L208.6,100.0 L212.4,100.0 L216.2,100.0 L220.0,100.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2099 \u2192 X</text><path d=\"M30.0,100.0 L33.8,100.0 L37.6,100.0 L41.4,100.0 L45.2,100.0 L49.0,100.0 L52.8,100.0 L56.6,100.0 L60.4,99.9 L64.2,99.9 L68.0,99.9 L71.8,99.8 L75.6,99.7 L79.4,99.5 L83.2,99.3 L87.0,99.0 L90.8,98.6 L94.6,98.1 L98.4,97.4 L102.2,96.5 L106.0,95.5 L109.8,94.2 L113.6,92.7 L117.4,91.0 L121.2,89.1 L125.0,87.0 L128.8,84.8 L132.6,82.5 L136.4,80.2 L140.2,78.1 L144.0,76.1 L147.8,74.5 L151.6,73.2 L155.4,72.4 L159.2,72.0 L163.0,72.2 L166.8,72.8 L170.6,73.9 L174.4,75.4 L178.2,77.2 L182.0,79.3 L185.8,81.5 L189.6,83.8 L193.4,86.1 L197.2,88.2 L201.0,90.2 L204.8,92.0 L208.6,93.6 L212.4,95.0 L216.2,96.1 L220.0,97.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">cX (c = 2)</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2099 \u2192 X in law, Y\u2099 \u2192 c in prob.</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2099 Y\u2099 \u2192 cX   and   X\u2099/Y\u2099 \u2192 X/c  (c \u2260 0)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">in-law limits can be rescaled by a probability limit</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The limit law X of X\u2099 and, dashed, the law of cX when Y\u2099 \u2192 c in probability: X\u2099\u00b7Y\u2099 \u2192 cX and X\u2099/Y\u2099 \u2192 X/c.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Continuous Mapping Theorem (CMT):</strong> If $X_n \\to X$ in mode (a.s., $P$, or $d$) and $g$ is continuous, then $g(X_n) \\to g(X)$ in the SAME mode.",
      "<strong>Slutsky\u2019s Theorem:</strong> If $X_n \\xrightarrow{d} X$ and $Y_n \\xrightarrow{P} c$ (where $c$ is a deterministic constant): (1) $X_n + Y_n \\xrightarrow{d} X + c$; (2) $X_n Y_n \\xrightarrow{d} c X$; (3) $X_n / Y_n \\xrightarrow{d} X / c$ (if $c \\ne 0$).",
      "<strong>Statistical Powerhouse:</strong> Slutsky allows replacing unknown nuisance parameters (like population variance $\\sigma$) with consistent sample estimates $S_n \\xrightarrow{P} \\sigma$."
    ],
    "math": "$$X_n \\xrightarrow{d} X, \\; Y_n \\xrightarrow{P} c \\implies X_n Y_n \\xrightarrow{d} cX, \\quad \\frac{X_n}{Y_n} \\xrightarrow{d} \\frac{X}{c} \\quad (c \\ne 0)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>A fixed number can ride along.</strong> If your average delay has a limiting bell shape and the typical ride length settles to a constant, the delay per minute of ride has the same bell shape scaled by that constant.",
    "examTrap": "Applying Slutsky when Y_n \u2192^d Y with non-constant Y! If Y is random, X_n + Y_n does NOT necessarily converge to X + Y because distribution convergence tells you nothing about mutual joint alignment.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Slutsky's theorem.</b> If $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X$ and $Y_n\\xrightarrow{P}c$ for a constant $c$, then $X_n+Y_n\\xrightarrow{d}X+c$, $X_nY_n\\xrightarrow{d}cX$, and $X_n/Y_n\\xrightarrow{d}X/c$ if $c\\ne0$. The continuous mapping theorem: if $g$ is continuous a.e. under the law of $X$ and $X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X$, then $g(X_n)\\xrightarrow{d}g(X)$ (Ross 8.3; Grimmett 7.2).",
    "hint": "If one record settles in shape and another settles to a number, how does their product behave?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$X_n\\xrightarrow{d}X$ = the first quantity settles in shape.</strong> Its histogram converges, say to a bell.",
      "<strong>$Y_n\\xrightarrow{P}c$ = the second settles to a number.</strong> It is close to the constant $c$ with chance tending to 1.",
      "<strong>$X_nY_n\\xrightarrow{d}cX$ = rescale the limit.</strong> Multiply the limit law by the constant; likewise dividing by $Y_n$ divides by $c$ (needs $c\\ne0$)."
    ],
