    "title": "The First Borel-Cantelli Lemma: Sum P(A_n) < \u221e \u21d2 P(A_n i.o.) = 0",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31602\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31602)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31602)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">n</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2119(A\u2099)</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"28.0\" width=\"12\" height=\"72.0\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"52\" y=\"82.0\" width=\"12\" height=\"18.0\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"70\" y=\"92.0\" width=\"12\" height=\"8.0\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"88\" y=\"95.5\" width=\"12\" height=\"4.5\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"106\" y=\"97.12\" width=\"12\" height=\"2.88\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"124\" y=\"98.0\" width=\"12\" height=\"2.0\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"142\" y=\"98.53061224489795\" width=\"12\" height=\"1.469387755102041\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"98.875\" width=\"12\" height=\"1.125\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"178\" y=\"99.11111111111111\" width=\"12\" height=\"0.8888888888888888\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"196\" y=\"99.28\" width=\"12\" height=\"0.72\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2211 \u2119(A\u2099) &lt; \u221e (here chances like 1/n\u00b2)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">the tail \u2211\u2096\u2265\u2099 \u2119(A\u2096) \u2192 0, so \u2119(late i.o.) \u2264 any tail = 0</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">no independence needed</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">after some day,</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">no more lateness</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Bars of \u2119(A\u2099) shrinking like 1/n\u00b2. The sum is finite, so the tail \u2211\u2096\u2265\u2099 \u2119(A\u2096) \u2192 0, and \u2119(A\u2099 infinitely often) = 0.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Theorem Statement:</strong> For ANY sequence of events $\\{A_n\\}$, if $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\mathbb{P}(A_n) \\lt  \\infty$, then $\\mathbb{P}(\\limsup A_n) = \\mathbb{P}(A_n \\text{ i.o.}) = 0$.",
      "<strong>Direct Union Bound Proof:</strong> By continuity of measure, $\\mathbb{P}(\\limsup A_n) \\le \\mathbb{P}(\\bigcup_{k=n}^\\infty A_k) \\le \\sum_{k=n}^\\infty \\mathbb{P}(A_k)$. As $n \\to \\infty$, the tail of a convergent series tends to 0.",
      "<strong>ZERO Independence Assumption:</strong> BC1 holds unconditionally for dependent, correlated, or adversarial sequences of events."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\mathbb{P}(A_n) \\lt  \\infty \\implies \\mathbb{P}\\left(\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} A_n\\right) = \\mathbb{P}(A_n \\text{ i.o.}) = 0 \\quad (\\text{NO independence needed})$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Bad luck that fades.</strong> If the chance of lateness on day $n$ shrinks quickly, say like $1/n^2$, then with probability 1 there will be only finitely many late days, however the days are linked.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming BC1 requires independence! BC1 is a pure measure-theoretic consequence of countable sub-additivity; it requires zero independence.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>First Borel\u2013Cantelli lemma.</b> If $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\mathbb P(A_n)\\lt \\infty$, then $\\mathbb P(A_n\\text{ i.o.})=\\mathbb P(\\limsup A_n)=0$. No independence is needed. Proof: $\\mathbb P(\\limsup A_n)\\le\\mathbb P(\\bigcup_{k\\ge n}A_k)\\le\\sum_{k\\ge n}\\mathbb P(A_k)\\to0$ (Grimmett 7.3; Ross 8.2).",
    "hint": "If the chances of being late on day n add up to a finite number, how often can you be late?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\sum\\mathbb P(A_n)\\lt \\infty$ = the chances are summable.</strong> Chances like $1/n^2$ add up to a finite number; the late-day chances die away fast enough.",
      "<strong>$\\sum_{k\\ge n}\\mathbb P(A_k)\\to0$ = the tail of the sum vanishes.</strong> Being late on some day from $n$ on has chance at most this tail, by Boole's inequality.",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb P(\\limsup A_n)=0$ = finitely many late days.</strong> This holds for any dependence between days; only the size of the chances matters."
    ],
