    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31703\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31703)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31703)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">n</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2119(|X\u0304\u2099 \u2212 \u03bc| \u2265 \u03b5)</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"7.6000000000000085\" width=\"12\" height=\"92.39999999999999\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"52\" y=\"76.9\" width=\"12\" height=\"23.099999999999998\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"70\" y=\"89.73333333333333\" width=\"12\" height=\"10.266666666666666\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"88\" y=\"94.225\" width=\"12\" height=\"5.7749999999999995\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"106\" y=\"96.304\" width=\"12\" height=\"3.6959999999999997\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"124\" y=\"97.43333333333334\" width=\"12\" height=\"2.5666666666666664\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"142\" y=\"98.11428571428571\" width=\"12\" height=\"1.8857142857142855\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"98.55625\" width=\"12\" height=\"1.4437499999999999\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"178\" y=\"98.85925925925926\" width=\"12\" height=\"1.1407407407407406\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"196\" y=\"99.076\" width=\"12\" height=\"0.9239999999999999\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"11\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c(X\u0304\u2099 \u2212 \u03bc)\u2074 \u2264 C/n\u00b2: summable (like 1/n\u00b2)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2211 C/(n\u00b2\u03b5\u2074) &lt; \u221e  \u21d2  BC1  \u21d2  X\u0304\u2099 \u2192 \u03bc a.s.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">fourth moment finite</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">keeps the tail small</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">(needs \ud835\udd3cX\u2074 &lt; \u221e; the sharp version needs only \ud835\udd3c|X| &lt; \u221e)</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The bound \u2119(|X\u0304\u2099 \u2212 \u03bc| \u2265 \u03b5) \u2264 C/(n\u00b2\u03b5\u2074), drawn as bars shrinking like 1/n\u00b2. The sum is finite, so Borel\u2013Cantelli gives X\u0304\u2099 \u2192 \u03bc almost surely.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>4th Moment Expansion:</strong> Assume $\\mathbb{E}[X^4] \\lt  \\infty$ and $\\mu = 0$. $\\mathbb{E}[(\\sum X_i)^4] = \\sum \\mathbb{E}[X_i^4] + 6\\sum_{i \\lt  j} \\mathbb{E}[X_i^2 X_j^2] = n \\mathbb{E}[X^4] + 3n(n-1)\\sigma^4 \\le C n^2$.",
      "<strong>Fourth Power Decay:</strong> $\\mathbb{E}[(\\bar{X}_n)^4] = \\frac{1}{n^4} \\mathbb{E}[(\\sum X_i)^4] \\le \\frac{C n^2}{n^4} = \\frac{C}{n^2}$.",
      "<strong>Summability via BC1:</strong> By Markov, $\\mathbb{P}(|\\bar{X}_n| \\ge \\epsilon) \\le \\frac{\\mathbb{E}[(\\bar{X}_n)^4]}{\\epsilon^4} \\le \\frac{C}{n^2 \\epsilon^4}$. Since $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{6} \\lt  \\infty$, BC1 proves $\\bar{X}_n \\xrightarrow{a.s.} 0$!"
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mathbb{E}\\left[(\\bar{X}_n - \\mu)^4\\right] \\le \\frac{C}{n^2} \\implies \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\mathbb{P}\\left(|\\bar{X}_n - \\mu| \\ge \\epsilon\\right) \\le \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{C}{n^2 \\epsilon^4} \\lt  \\infty \\implies \\bar{X}_n \\xrightarrow{\\text{a.s.}} \\mu$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Why misses stop for good.</strong> The chance that day $n$'s average is off by a minute shrinks like $1/n^2$. Those chances add up to a finite number, so there is a last day on which you are off by that much.",
    "examTrap": "Chebyshev gives 1/n decay: \u2211 1/n = \u221e (diverges), which CANNOT be plugged into BC1! The 4th moment is essential to force the series to converge.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Strong law under a fourth moment.</b> If $X_i$ are i.i.d. with $\\mathbb E[X^4]\\lt \\infty$, then $\\mathbb E[(\\bar X_n-\\mu)^4]\\le C/n^2$, so $\\sum_n\\mathbb P(|\\bar X_n-\\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\sum_n\\frac{C}{n^2\\varepsilon^4}\\lt \\infty$, and by the first Borel\u2013Cantelli lemma $\\bar X_n\\to\\mu$ almost surely (Grimmett 7.5; Durrett 2.4).",
    "hint": "How does a finite fourth moment make the chances of missing the mean add up to a finite number?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[(\\bar X_n-\\mu)^4]\\le C/n^2$ = the fourth moment of the average.</strong> Expanding the fourth power, only $O(n^2)$ products of centred terms survive, out of $n^4$, and dividing by $n^4$ leaves $C/n^2$.",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb P(|\\bar X_n-\\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\frac{C}{n^2\\varepsilon^4}$ = Markov on the fourth power.</strong> A fourth-moment bound turns into a bound on the chance of a miss.",
      "<strong>$\\sum1/n^2\\lt \\infty\\Rightarrow$ BC1 $\\Rightarrow$ a.s.</strong> Summable chances give finitely many misses, so the running average converges a.s."
    ],
