    "cardNumber": "06",
    "category": "WORKED BENCHMARK",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "Worked Benchmark: Catastrophic Failure of the Law of Large Numbers (Cauchy)",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31706\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31706)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31706)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">n</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">x\u0304\u2099</text><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><path d=\"M29.0,64.9 L34.0,63.9 L39.0,55.7 L44.0,57.9 L49.0,59.1 L54.0,59.6 L59.0,61.6 L64.0,61.8 L69.0,61.8 L74.0,61.2 L79.0,63.3 L84.0,63.7 L89.0,65.3 L94.0,64.6 L99.0,64.3 L104.0,67.1 L109.0,60.6 L114.0,57.8 L119.0,57.9 L124.0,58.1 L129.0,58.9 L134.0,65.0 L139.0,64.9 L144.0,66.2 L149.0,66.4 L154.0,66.6 L159.0,68.8 L164.0,68.7 L169.0,68.6 L174.0,68.0 L179.0,67.9 L184.0,67.7 L189.0,67.6 L194.0,67.4 L199.0,67.3 L204.0,67.3 L209.0,45.2 L214.0,34.1 L219.0,34.5 L224.0,35.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">Cauchy days: no mean exists</text><text x=\"119.52\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c6(X\u0304\u2099)(t) = [e^(\u2212|t|/n)]\u207f = e^(\u2212|t|): X\u0304\u2099 has the same law as X\u2081</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">averaging never helps: the average never settles</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The running average of Cauchy days keeps jumping instead of settling at a mean, because \u03c6(X\u0304\u2099)(t) = e^(\u2212|t|) for every n: the average is no tamer than one day.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Problem Setup:</strong> Let $X_1, \\dots, X_n \\stackrel{\\text{i.i.d.}}{\\sim} \\text{Cauchy}(0, 1)$ with density $f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\pi(1+x^2)}$. Its characteristic function is $\\phi_X(t) = e^{-|t|}$. First moment $\\mathbb{E}[|X|] = \\infty$.",
      "<strong>Sample Mean Characteristic Function:</strong> $\\phi_{\\bar{X}_n}(t) = [\\phi_X(t/n)]^n = [e^{-|t|/n}]^n = e^{-|t|} = \\phi_X(t)$!",
      "<strong>Zero Averaging Gain:</strong> The sample mean $\\bar{X}_n$ is identically distributed to $X_1$ for every $n$. Averaging 1,000,000 Cauchy variables does NOT concentrate around 0; both WLLN and SLLN fail completely!"
    ],
    "math": "$$\\phi_{\\bar{X}_n}(t) = \\left[\\exp\\left(-\\frac{|t|}{n}\\right)\\right]^n = \\exp(-|t|) \\implies \\bar{X}_n \\stackrel{d}{=} X_1 \\quad \\forall n \\ge 1$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Days with freak outliers.</strong> If occasionally a day is so extreme that it dwarfs everything before it, the running average keeps being thrown off, however many days you average.",
    "examTrap": "Attempting to apply LLN to Cauchy distributions because the PDF looks symmetric! Symmetry is irrelevant when the tail integral \u222b |x| f(x)dx diverges to infinity.",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Failure for the Cauchy law.</b> If $X_i$ are i.i.d. standard Cauchy, $\\phi_X(t)=e^{-|t|}$, so $\\phi_{\\bar X_n}(t)=\\bigl[e^{-|t|/n}\\bigr]^n=e^{-|t|}$ and $\\bar X_n\\overset d=X_1$ for every $n$. Hence $\\bar X_n$ does not converge to a constant: the laws of large numbers fail since $\\mathbb E|X|=\\infty$ (Grimmett 5.10).",
    "hint": "What happens to the average if single days can be so extreme that no mean exists?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\phi_{\\bar X_n}(t)=\\bigl[\\phi_X(t/n)\\bigr]^n$ = the average's characteristic function.</strong> Independence turns the sum into a product of $n$ equal factors.",
      "<strong>$\\bigl[e^{-|t|/n}\\bigr]^n=e^{-|t|}$ = nothing changes.</strong> The $n$ factors combine exactly back to $e^{-|t|}$, the characteristic function of one day.",
      "<strong>$\\bar X_n\\overset d=X_1$ = averaging does not help.</strong> The average of $n$ days is as wild as one day, so it never settles."
    ],
