    "id": 31801,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "18_Characteristic_Functions_and_Central_Limit_Theorem",
    "topicTitle": "18 \u00b7 Characteristic Functions & CLT",
    "cardNumber": "01",
    "category": "FOURIER TRANSFORM",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "The Characteristic Function: Universal Fourier Dual",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31801\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"64.0\" r=\"40.0\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"26\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"114\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><circle cx=\"107.58770483143634\" cy=\"50.319194266973255\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"76.94592710667722\" cy=\"24.607689879511682\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"35.35898384862245\" cy=\"44.0\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"32.41229516856366\" cy=\"77.68080573302674\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"63.05407289332279\" cy=\"103.39231012048832\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"100.64177772475911\" cy=\"89.71150438746159\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"76\" cy=\"62\" r=\"2.8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"84\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c6(t)</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">e^(itX) on the unit circle</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c6(t) = \ud835\udd3c[e^(itX)]</text><text x=\"119.52\" y=\"54\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">average of points on the circle,</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">so it lies inside: |\u03c6| \u2264 1</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c6(aX + b)(t) = e^(itb) \u03c6(at)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">exists for every law, even without a mean</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">(each day is a point on the circle: a spin by t\u00b7time)</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Samples of e^(itX) are points on the unit circle. Their average \u03c6(t) = \ud835\udd3c[e^(itX)] lies inside the circle, so |\u03c6| \u2264 1 always; it encodes the whole law.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Definition:</strong> $\\phi_X(t) \\triangleq \\mathbb{E}[e^{itX}] = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{itx} dF_X(x)$ for all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$.",
      "<strong>Universal Existence:</strong> Because $|e^{itX}| = |\\cos(tX) + i\\sin(tX)| = 1$, the integral $\\int |e^{itx}| dF(x) = \\int 1 dF(x) = 1 \\lt  \\infty$ ALWAYS converges. Unlike MGFs, $\\phi_X(t)$ exists for every distribution.",
      "<strong>Fundamental Properties:</strong> (1) $\\phi_X(0) = 1$; (2) $|\\phi_X(t)| \\le 1$; (3) $\\phi_X(-t) = \\overline{\\phi_X(t)}$; (4) Uniformly continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$; (5) $\\phi_{aX+b}(t) = e^{itb}\\phi_X(at)$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\phi_X(t) = \\mathbb{E}\\left[e^{itX}\\right], \\qquad |\\phi_X(t)| \\le \\mathbb{E}\\left[|e^{itX}|\\right] = 1 \\quad \\forall t \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad \\phi_{aX+b}(t) = e^{itb}\\phi_X(at)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Wrapping the ride time around a circle.</strong> Each possible ride length becomes a direction on a circle. The average direction, over all mornings, is a point inside the circle that encodes the whole law of the ride time.",
    "examTrap": "Believing characteristic functions can diverge to infinity like MGFs! Because |e^{itx}| = 1, the characteristic function is universally bounded by 1 everywhere.",
    "stmtKind": "DEFINITION",
    "statement": "<b>Characteristic function.</b> For a random variable $X$, $\\varphi_X(t)=\\mathbb E[e^{itX}]$, $t\\in\\mathbb R$. It exists for every $X$, $|\\varphi_X(t)|\\le1$, $\\varphi_X(0)=1$, $\\varphi_{aX+b}(t)=e^{itb}\\varphi_X(at)$, and it determines the law of $X$; for independent $X,Y$, $\\varphi_{X+Y}=\\varphi_X\\varphi_Y$ (Grimmett 5.7; Durrett 3.3).",
    "hint": "Which transform of a ride-time law always exists, even for laws with no mean?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$e^{itX}$ = a point on the unit circle.</strong> Each day's time $X$ becomes an angle $tX$ and a point on a circle of radius 1.",
      "<strong>$\\varphi_X(t)=\\mathbb E[e^{itX}]$ = the average of those points.</strong> An average of points on the circle lies inside it, so $|\\varphi_X(t)|\\le1$ and it always exists.",
      "<strong>$\\varphi_{aX+b}(t)=e^{itb}\\varphi_X(at)$ and $\\varphi_{X+Y}=\\varphi_X\\varphi_Y$.</strong> Shifts rotate, scalings stretch $t$, and independent sums multiply; this is the Fourier dual of the law."
    ],
