    "id": 31805,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "18_Characteristic_Functions_and_Central_Limit_Theorem",
    "topicTitle": "18 \u00b7 Characteristic Functions & CLT",
    "cardNumber": "05",
    "category": "ADVANCED CLT",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "Lindeberg vs Lyapunov Conditions for Non-Identical Sums",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31805\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31805)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31805)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">leg i</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c3\u1d62\u00b2</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"82\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"64\" y=\"78\" width=\"16\" height=\"22\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"88\" y=\"84\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"112\" y=\"76\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"136\" y=\"80\" width=\"16\" height=\"20\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"82\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"184\" y=\"78\" width=\"16\" height=\"22\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">no single leg dominates the total spread</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Lyapunov: \u2211\ud835\udd3c|X\u1d62 \u2212 \u03bc\u1d62|^(2+\u03b4) / (\u2211\u03c3\u1d62\u00b2)^((2+\u03b4)/2) \u2192 0</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u21d2 (\u2211(X\u1d62 \u2212 \u03bc\u1d62))/\u221a(\u2211\u03c3\u1d62\u00b2) \u2192 \ud835\udca9(0,1)</text><text x=\"196\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">balanced: bell</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Variances \u03c3\u1d62\u00b2 of seven different legs. If they are balanced and no leg dominates, the Lyapunov ratio tends to 0 and the standardised total is asymptotically \ud835\udca9(0,1).",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Setup:</strong> Let $X_i$ be independent with means $\\mu_i$ and variances $\\sigma_i^2$. Total variance is $s_n^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sigma_i^2$.",
      "<strong>Lindeberg Condition:</strong> For all $\\epsilon > 0$, $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{s_n^2} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbb{E}[(X_i - \\mu_i)^2 \\mathbf{1}_{\\{|X_i - \\mu_i| \\ge \\epsilon s_n\\}}] = 0$. Guarantees $\\frac{\\sum (X_i - \\mu_i)}{s_n} \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$.",
      "<strong>Lyapunov Condition:</strong> If $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{s_n^{2+\\delta}} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbb{E}[|X_i - \\mu_i|^{2+\\delta}] = 0$ for some $\\delta > 0$, then Lindeberg's condition holds automatically."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\text{Lyapunov: } \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbb{E}[|X_i - \\mu_i|^{2+\\delta}]}{\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n \\sigma_i^2\\right)^{(2+\\delta)/2}} = 0 \\implies \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu_i)}{\\sqrt{\\sum \\sigma_i^2}} \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Many different small legs.</strong> A trip made of different legs (walk, wait, ride, transfer), each with its own spread, still adds up to a bell, as long as no single leg is so big or so wild that it dominates the total.",
    "examTrap": "Applying CLT when one giant term dominates the sum! If X\u2081 contributes 90% of total variance, the sum reflects X\u2081's individual distribution and fails CLT.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Lyapunov and Lindeberg CLT.</b> Let $X_i$ be independent with means $\\mu_i$, variances $\\sigma_i^2$ and $s_n^2=\\sum_{i\\le n}\\sigma_i^2$. If for some $\\delta\\gt 0$ (Lyapunov) $\\frac1{s_n^{2+\\delta}}\\sum_{i\\le n}\\mathbb E|X_i-\\mu_i|^{2+\\delta}\\to0$, then $\\frac1{s_n}\\sum_{i\\le n}(X_i-\\mu_i)\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal N(0,1)$. Lindeberg's condition $\\frac1{s_n^2}\\sum\\mathbb E[(X_i-\\mu_i)^2\\mathbf 1_{\\{|X_i-\\mu_i|\\gt \\varepsilon s_n\\}}]\\to0$ is weaker and, with $\\max_i\\sigma_i/s_n\\to0$, necessary (Grimmett 5.10; Durrett 3.4).",
    "hint": "If the days or legs are not identical, what stops a single one from spoiling the bell?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$s_n^2=\\sum\\sigma_i^2$ = the total variance.</strong> The sum of every leg's variance.",
      "<strong>$\\sum\\mathbb E|X_i-\\mu_i|^{2+\\delta}\\big/s_n^{2+\\delta}\\to0$ = no leg dominates.</strong> The (2+\u03b4)-th moments are small compared with the total spread, so no single big jump drives the sum.",
      "<strong>Lindeberg = the weaker, sharper version.</strong> Only the tails beyond $\\varepsilon s_n$ are required to contribute nothing in the limit; Lyapunov implies Lindeberg."
    ],
    "textbookImg": null,
    "textbookRef": null
