    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "Ergodic Theorem & Strong Law for DTMCs",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32006\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32006)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32006)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">N days</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">fraction late</text><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"88\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><text x=\"224\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c0(L) = \u2153</text><path d=\"M27.3,108.0 L30.6,108.0 L33.9,88.0 L37.2,93.0 L40.5,96.0 L43.8,98.0 L47.1,99.4 L50.4,100.5 L53.7,101.3 L57.0,102.0 L60.3,102.5 L63.6,98.0 L66.9,94.2 L70.2,95.1 L73.5,96.0 L76.8,96.8 L80.1,97.4 L83.4,98.0 L86.7,98.5 L90.0,99.0 L93.3,96.6 L96.6,97.1 L99.9,97.6 L103.2,98.0 L106.5,98.4 L109.8,98.8 L113.1,99.1 L116.4,99.4 L119.7,99.7 L123.0,98.0 L126.3,98.3 L129.6,96.8 L132.9,95.3 L136.2,93.9 L139.5,92.6 L142.8,93.0 L146.1,93.4 L149.4,93.8 L152.7,94.2 L156.0,94.5 L159.3,94.8 L162.6,95.1 L165.9,95.4 L169.2,95.7 L172.5,96.0 L175.8,95.0 L179.1,94.0 L182.4,93.0 L185.7,92.1 L189.0,91.2 L192.3,90.4 L195.6,89.5 L198.9,88.8 L202.2,88.0 L205.5,88.4 L208.8,88.7 L212.1,89.1 L215.4,89.4 L218.7,89.7 L222.0,90.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">(1/N) \u2211 f(X\u2099) \u2192 \u2211\u2c7c \u03c0\u2c7c f(j) = \ud835\udd3c_\u03c0[f]  almost surely</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The fraction of late mornings along one long run converges to \u03c0(L) = \u2153: the time average along a single path equals the average under the stationary split.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Strong Law for DTMCs:</strong> Let $\\{X_n\\}$ be an irreducible positive-recurrent Markov chain with stationary distribution $\\pi$. For any function $f: S \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\sum_i |f(i)| \\pi_i \\lt  \\infty$, the sample path time average converges almost surely: $\\lim_{N \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{n=1}^N f(X_n) = \\sum_{i \\in S} f(i) \\pi_i$.",
      "<strong>Occupancy Fraction Representation:</strong> Setting indicator reward $f(k) = \\mathbf{1}_{\\{k = j\\}}$, the Ergodic Theorem establishes that the empirical fraction of time spent in state $j$ converges almost surely to $\\pi_j$: $\\lim_{N \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{n=1}^N \\mathbf{1}_{\\{X_n = j\\}} = \\pi_j$.",
      "<strong>Long-Run Cost Evaluation:</strong> In queuing and buffer engineering, long-run packet drop rates, average buffer occupancy $\\mathbb{E}[L]$, and operational running costs are computed directly via stationary expectations $\\sum_i f(i) \\pi_i$ without tracking transient distributions."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\lim_{N \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{n=1}^N f(X_n) = \\sum_{j \\in S} \\pi_j f(j) = \\mathbb{E}_\\pi[f(X)] \\quad \\text{almost surely}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>One term tells the story.</strong> Keep a tally of how many mornings were late. After a long term, the fraction of late mornings is very close to $\\frac13$, just as the steady split says, whatever happened on day one.",
    "examTrap": "Applying the Ergodic Theorem to reducible chains! If the chain has multiple communicating recurrent classes, the long-run average lim (1/N) \u03a3 f(X_n) is a random variable dependent on which class the chain was absorbed into, not a single constant!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Ergodic theorem for Markov chains.</b> For an irreducible positive recurrent chain with stationary $\\pi$ and $f$ with $\\sum_j\\pi_j|f(j)|\\lt \\infty$, $\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac1N\\sum_{n=1}^Nf(X_n)=\\sum_{j\\in S}\\pi_jf(j)=\\mathbb E_\\pi[f(X)]$ almost surely, from any starting state (Ross 4.4; Grimmett 6.4).",
    "hint": "Does one long run of commutes show the steady shares, without averaging over many runs?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\frac1N\\sum_{n\\le N}f(X_n)$ = a time average on one run.</strong> Follow one run of days and average a quantity $f$ of each day's state.",
      "<strong>$\\sum_j\\pi_jf(j)$ = the average under $\\pi$.</strong> The same quantity averaged over the stationary split.",
      "<strong>almost surely = every run.</strong> This is the strong law for dependent days: time averages equal space averages."
    ],
