    "id": 32205,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "22_Birth_Death_Chains_and_MCMC",
    "topicTitle": "22 \u00b7 Birth-Death Chains & MCMC",
    "cardNumber": "05",
    "category": "MCMC TUNING",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "Random Walk Metropolis & The Proposal Tuning Dilemma",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32205\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32205)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32205)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">x</text><path d=\"M28.0,90.0 L31.3,90.0 L34.5,90.0 L37.8,90.0 L41.1,90.0 L44.3,89.9 L47.6,89.9 L50.9,89.8 L54.1,89.7 L57.4,89.6 L60.7,89.3 L63.9,88.9 L67.2,88.4 L70.5,87.6 L73.7,86.5 L77.0,85.0 L80.3,83.1 L83.5,80.6 L86.8,77.5 L90.1,73.9 L93.3,69.6 L96.6,64.8 L99.9,59.6 L103.1,54.2 L106.4,48.9 L109.7,43.8 L112.9,39.4 L116.2,35.8 L119.5,33.3 L122.7,32.1 L126.0,32.3 L129.3,33.8 L132.5,36.5 L135.8,40.3 L139.1,44.9 L142.3,50.1 L145.6,55.5 L148.9,60.8 L152.1,65.9 L155.4,70.6 L158.7,74.7 L161.9,78.3 L165.2,81.2 L168.5,83.6 L171.7,85.4 L175.0,86.8 L178.3,87.8 L181.5,88.5 L184.8,89.0 L188.1,89.4 L191.3,89.6 L194.6,89.8 L197.9,89.9 L201.1,89.9 L204.4,90.0 L207.7,90.0 L210.9,90.0 L214.2,90.0 L217.5,90.0 L220.7,90.0 L224.0,90.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"118\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"109\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">tiny</text><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"183\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">huge</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03b1(x,y) = min(1, \u03c0(y)/\u03c0(x)) for a symmetric proposal</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">tiny steps: accepted often,</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">but you barely move</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">huge steps: you move,</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">but are mostly rejected</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">optimal acceptance \u2248 23.4% in high dimension</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The target and two step sizes: tiny steps are accepted but crawl, huge steps are rejected. The best compromise accepts about 23.4% of proposals.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Symmetric Proposal Simplification:</strong> When proposal distribution is symmetric ($q(y \\mid x) = q(x \\mid y)$, e.g., Gaussian $y \\sim \\mathcal{N}(x, \\sigma^2)$ or Uniform $y \\sim \\text{Unif}[x-\\delta, x+\\delta]$), the proposal density ratio equals 1, yielding classic Metropolis: $\\alpha(x, y) = \\min\\left(1, \\frac{\\pi(y)}{\\pi(x)}\\right)$.",
      "<strong>Step-Size Goldilocks Dilemma:</strong> If proposal step $\\delta$ is too small, nearly all moves are accepted but the chain diffuses sluggishly (high autocorrelation). If $\\delta$ is too large, proposed jumps land in low-probability tails and are overwhelmingly rejected, trapping the chain.",
      "<strong>Optimal Acceptance Rate Benchmark:</strong> For high-dimensional Gaussian targets ($d \\to \\infty$), Gelman, Roberts & Gilks proved the optimal acceptance rate that maximizes asymptotic diffusion efficiency is approximately $23.4\\%$ (and $\\approx 44\\%$ in 1D)."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\alpha(x, y) = \\min\\left(1, \\frac{\\pi(y)}{\\pi(x)}\\right) \\quad (\\text{Symmetric: } q(y \\mid x) = q(x \\mid y)); \\quad \\text{Optimal Accept Rate} \\approx 23.4\\%$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Choosing the step.</strong> Trying a tiny change to your departure time is almost always accepted but explores slowly. A wild change is almost always rejected. A medium change explores best.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming an acceptance rate near 100% indicates superior MCMC performance! A near-100% acceptance rate usually means the proposal step size is infinitesimally tiny, leaving the sampler trapped in a microscopic corner of the state space!",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Random-walk Metropolis.</b> With a symmetric proposal, $q(y\\mid x)=q(x\\mid y)$, the acceptance is $\\alpha(x,y)=\\min\\bigl(1,\\frac{\\pi(y)}{\\pi(x)}\\bigr)$. The step size trades off acceptance against movement; for Gaussian-like targets in high dimension the optimal acceptance rate is about $23.4\\%$ (Roberts\u2013Gelman\u2013Gilks).",
    "hint": "How big should a proposed step be: small and safe, or large and risky?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$q(y\\mid x)=q(x\\mid y)$ = symmetric proposal.</strong> A step of a given size forwards is as likely as backwards, so the $q$ factors cancel.",
      "<strong>$\\alpha=\\min(1,\\pi(y)/\\pi(x))$ = compare weights only.</strong> Always go uphill; go downhill with the ratio of heights.",
      "<strong>$\\approx23.4\\%$ = the optimal acceptance.</strong> Tiny steps are accepted but barely move; huge steps move but are rejected; the best compromise accepts about one in four."
    ],
