    "highlighterClass": "highlight-purple",
    "title": "Poisson Thinning (Splitting) & Superposition (Merging)",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32404\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"98\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"98\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"98\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"102\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"118\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"102\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"118\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">all buses, rate \u03bb  (merge: \u03bb\u2081 + \u03bb\u2082)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">route 1: PP(\u03bbp)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">route 2: PP(\u03bb(1 \u2212 p))</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">split: independent</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">Poisson streams</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "One stream of rate \u03bb split by independent coin flips into route 1 with rate \u03bbp and route 2 with rate \u03bb(1 \u2212 p), which are independent. Merging reverses this.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Superposition (Merging):</strong> If $N_1(t)$ and $N_2(t)$ are independent Poisson processes with rates $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, their sum $N(t) = N_1(t) + N_2(t)$ is a Poisson process with aggregate rate $\\lambda = \\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2$.",
      "<strong>Bernoulli Thinning (Splitting):</strong> If each event of a Poisson process $N(t) \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda)$ is independently classified as Type 1 with probability $p$ and Type 2 with probability $1-p$, the resulting subprocesses $N_1(t)$ and $N_2(t)$ are Poisson processes with rates $\\lambda p$ and $\\lambda (1-p)$.",
      "<strong>Surprising Independence of Subprocesses:</strong> Remarkably, the split processes $N_1(t)$ and $N_2(t)$ are completely *independent* random variables for all $t$, despite both originating from the exact same parent process $N(t)$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\text{Merge: } N_1 + N_2 \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2); \\quad \\text{Split: } N_1 \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda p) \\ \\perp \\ N_2 \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda(1-p))$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Routes of one line.</strong> If 6 buses an hour are each independently route A with chance 1/3, route A buses form their own random stream of 2 an hour, independent of the route B stream of 4.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming split streams N_1 and N_2 must be correlated because N_1 + N_2 = N! For a fixed total N=n, they are dependent Binomials. But when total count N is Poisson, the random total decouples them into strictly independent Poisson processes!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Superposition and thinning.</b> If $N_1,N_2$ are independent Poisson processes with rates $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2$, then $N_1+N_2$ is Poisson with rate $\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2$ (merging). If each point of a rate-$\\lambda$ Poisson process is independently kept with probability $p$, the kept points $N_1\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda p)$ and the rest $N_2\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda(1-p))$ are independent (Ross 5.3; Grimmett 6.8).",
    "hint": "What happens when two bus streams merge, or when one stream is split among two routes?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$N_1+N_2\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$ = merge.</strong> Two independent Poisson streams combine into one Poisson stream whose rate is the sum.",
      "<strong>$N_1\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda p)$ = thin.</strong> Keep each arrival independently with chance $p$; the kept stream is Poisson with rate $\\lambda p$.",
      "<strong>$N_1\\perp N_2$ = the split streams are independent.</strong> Knowing how many buses took route 1 tells you nothing about route 2."
    ],
