    "id": 32406,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "24_Poisson_Processes_Properties_and_Extensions",
    "topicTitle": "24 \u00b7 Poisson Processes & Extensions",
    "cardNumber": "06",
    "category": "RENEWAL PARADOX",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "The Inspection Paradox & Length-Biased Sampling",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32406\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"54\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"54\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><circle cx=\"148\" cy=\"54\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"148\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">you arrive</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"78\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">the gap that holds you</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">you land in a longer-than-average gap</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c[wait W] = 1/\u03bb,  \ud835\udd3c[time since last B] \u2192 1/\u03bb</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c[gap around you] \u2192 2/\u03bb = 2\ud835\udd3c[T]</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">long gaps cover more of the clock, so you land in them more</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Buses on a time line and your arrival time inside a gap. Long gaps are more likely to hold your arrival: the gap around you averages 2/\u03bb, twice the typical gap.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Forward & Backward Recurrence Times:</strong> At fixed time $t$, let $W(t)$ be time until next arrival (forward recurrence) and $B(t)$ be time since last arrival (backward recurrence). By memorylessness, $W(t) \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ with $\\mathbb{E}[W(t)] = 1/\\lambda$, and as $t \\to \\infty$, $B(t) \\xrightarrow{d} \\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$.",
      "<strong>The Inspection Paradox:</strong> The total length of the renewal interval covering time $t$ is $I(t) = W(t) + B(t)$. Its asymptotic expectation is $\\mathbb{E}[I(t)] = \\frac{1}{\\lambda} + \\frac{1}{\\lambda} = \\frac{2}{\\lambda} = 2 \\mathbb{E}[T]$, exactly twice the average inter-arrival time!",
      "<strong>Length-Biased Sampling Mechanism:</strong> A randomly selected point $t$ is more likely to fall into an unusually wide inter-arrival gap than a narrow one, creating a length-biased sampling density $f_{\\text{observed}}(x) = \\frac{x f(x)}{\\mathbb{E}[T]} = \\lambda^2 x e^{-\\lambda x}$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mathbb{E}[W(t)] = \\frac{1}{\\lambda}, \\quad \\mathbb{E}[B(t)] \\to \\frac{1}{\\lambda} \\implies \\mathbb{E}[I(t)] = \\mathbb{E}[W(t) + B(t)] \\to \\frac{2}{\\lambda} = 2 \\mathbb{E}[T]$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Why you always wait.</strong> If the average gap between buses is 10 minutes, the gap you walk into averages 20 minutes. You wait 10 minutes on average, and your last bus left 10 minutes ago.",
    "examTrap": "Thinking E[W(t) + B(t)] must equal E[T]! An arrival chosen by fixed time t does NOT select an interval uniformly at random. It samples with probability proportional to interval duration x, producing the classic length-biased factor 2!",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Inspection paradox.</b> For a Poisson process of rate $\\lambda$ and a fixed time $t$, the wait $W(t)$ to the next arrival has $\\mathbb E[W(t)]=\\frac1\\lambda$, and the time $B(t)$ since the last one has $\\mathbb E[B(t)]\\to\\frac1\\lambda$. So the gap containing $t$ has $\\mathbb E[I(t)]=\\mathbb E[W+B]\\to\\frac2\\lambda=2\\mathbb E[T]$: length-biased sampling (Ross 7.7; Grimmett 10.4).",
    "hint": "Why does the gap you happen to arrive in tend to be longer than the average gap?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[W(t)]=\\frac1\\lambda$ = the wait for the next bus.</strong> By memorylessness it is the same as the average gap.",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[B(t)]\\to\\frac1\\lambda$ = the time since the last bus.</strong> Looking backwards from a typical time, the age is again exponential in the long run.",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[I(t)]\\to\\frac2\\lambda=2\\mathbb E[T]$ = a double-length gap.</strong> Long gaps cover more of the clock, so a random arrival time lands in them more often."
    ],
