    "highlighterClass": "highlight-purple",
    "title": "Quadratic Variation [B]_t = t & The Fundamental It\u00f4 Rule",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32504\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32504)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32504)\"/><path d=\"M26.0,64.0 L29.9,61.0 L33.8,64.0 L37.8,61.0 L41.7,64.0 L45.6,61.0 L49.5,58.0 L53.4,61.0 L57.4,58.0 L61.3,61.0 L65.2,58.0 L69.1,55.0 L73.0,52.0 L77.0,49.0 L80.9,52.0 L84.8,49.0 L88.7,46.0 L92.6,43.0 L96.6,40.0 L100.5,37.0 L104.4,40.0 L108.3,37.0 L112.2,34.0 L116.2,31.0 L120.1,34.0 L124.0,31.0 L127.9,34.0 L131.8,31.0 L135.8,34.0 L139.7,31.0 L143.6,34.0 L147.5,31.0 L151.4,34.0 L155.4,37.0 L159.3,40.0 L163.2,37.0 L167.1,34.0 L171.0,37.0 L175.0,34.0 L178.9,31.0 L182.8,34.0 L186.7,37.0 L190.6,40.0 L194.6,43.0 L198.5,40.0 L202.4,37.0 L206.3,40.0 L210.2,37.0 L214.2,40.0 L218.1,37.0 L222.0,40.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"26\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"26\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"76\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"76\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"124\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"124\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"174\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"174\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"222\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"26\" y1=\"104\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"104\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">cut [0, t] into tiny pieces, square each move</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"119\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2211 (\u0394B)\u00b2 \u2192 t in L\u00b2</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"131\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">so (dB)\u00b2 = dt and dB\u00b7dt = 0</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">the squares add up to the elapsed time, not to 0</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A partition of [0, t] and the path's increments. The sum of squared increments tends to t: this is the quadratic variation [B]\u209c = t, the origin of the extra term in It\u00f4's formula.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Quadratic Variation Theorem:</strong> For any partition $\\Pi_n = \\{0 = t_0 \\lt  \\dots \\lt  t_n = t\\}$ with mesh $\\|\\Pi_n\\| \\to 0$, the quadratic variation converges in $L^2$ to the deterministic value $t$: $[B, B]_t = \\lim_{\\|\\Pi_n\\| \\to 0} \\sum_{i=1}^n (B(t_i) - B(t_{i-1}))^2 = t$.",
      "<strong>Zero Asymptotic Variance:</strong> Because $\\Delta B_i \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\Delta t_i)$, $\\operatorname{Var}((\\Delta B_i)^2) = 2(\\Delta t_i)^2$. The total variance $\\sum 2(\\Delta t_i)^2 \\le 2 \\|\\Pi\\| t \\to 0$, causing all random fluctuations to vanish completely in the limit.",
      "<strong>Infinite Total Variation:</strong> Since quadratic variation is finite and non-zero ($t > 0$), the first-order total variation $\\sum |\\Delta B_i|$ diverges to $\\infty$, necessitating the second-order Taylor correction $(dB)^2 = dt$ in It\u00f4's formula."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\lim_{\\|\\Pi\\| \\to 0} \\sum_{i=1}^n (B(t_i) - B(t_{i-1}))^2 = t \\quad \\text{in } L^2 \\implies (dB)^2 = dt, \\quad dB \\cdot dt = 0$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Jitter does not cancel.</strong> The little squared wiggles in your delay add up to a fixed amount equal to the elapsed time. An ordinary smooth path would give zero, which is why a new calculus is needed.",
    "examTrap": "Applying ordinary chain rule d(f(B)) = f'(B) dB! Because (dB)\u00b2 = dt \u2260 0, It\u00f4's lemma requires the second-order term: df(B) = f'(B) dB + (1/2) f''(B) dt. Omitting (1/2)f'' dt is the most notorious error in stochastic calculus!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Quadratic variation and It\u00f4's rule.</b> For partitions $\\Pi$ of $[0,t]$, $\\lim_{\\|\\Pi\\|\\to0}\\sum_i\\bigl(B(t_i)-B(t_{i-1})\\bigr)^2=t$ in $L^2$, i.e. $[B]_t=t$. Formally $(dB)^2=dt$ and $dB\\,dt=0$, which gives It\u00f4's formula $df(B)=f'(B)dB+\\frac12f''(B)dt$ (\u00d8ksendal 4; Durrett 8).",
    "hint": "Why do the squared little moves of a jagged path add up to the elapsed time rather than to zero?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\sum(\\Delta B)^2$ = add up the squared moves.</strong> Chop $[0,t]$ into tiny pieces and square each piece's change.",
      "<strong>$\\to t$ in $L^2$ = the time elapsed.</strong> Each square has mean $\\Delta t$, and the variance of the sum goes to 0 as the mesh shrinks.",
      "<strong>$(dB)^2=dt$ and $dB\\,dt=0$ = the It\u00f4 rule.</strong> The second-order term $\\frac12f''\\,dt$ appears in the change of $f(B)$."
    ],
