" y=\"130\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03bc* = K*\u1d40 (K + \u03c3\u00b2I)\u207b\u00b9 y,  \u03a3* = K** \u2212 K*\u1d40 (K + \u03c3\u00b2I)\u207b\u00b9 K*</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"142\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">K(x, x\u2032) = \u03c3f\u00b2 e^(\u2212\u2016x \u2212 x\u2032\u2016\u00b2/2\u2113\u00b2)</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Four noisy readings, the posterior mean curve and a band of uncertainty. The band is narrow near the data and widens away from it.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Gaussian Process Definition:</strong> A collection of random variables $\\{f(x)\\}$ is a Gaussian Process $f(x) \\sim \\mathcal{GP}(m(x), K(x, x'))$ if every finite subset $(f(x_1), \\dots, f(x_n))$ has a joint multivariate Gaussian distribution.",
      "<strong>Closed-Form Posterior Conditioning:</strong> Given noisy training points $y = f(X) + \\epsilon$ with $\\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2 I)$, the posterior distribution at test point $x_*$ is Gaussian $\\mathcal{N}(\\mu_*, \\Sigma_*)$ with analytical mean $\\mu_* = K_*^\\top (K + \\sigma^2 I)^{-1} y$ and variance $\\Sigma_* = K_{**} - K_*^\\top (K + \\sigma^2 I)^{-1} K_*$.",
      "<strong>Squared Exponential Kernel:</strong> The ubiquitous RBF kernel $K(x, x') = \\sigma_f^2 \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\|x - x'\\|^2}{2\\ell^2}\\right)$ produces infinitely differentiable, smooth sample paths with characteristic length-scale $\\ell$ controlling function wiggliness."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mu_* = K_*^\\top (K + \\sigma^2 I)^{-1} y, \\qquad \\Sigma_* = K_{**} - K_*^\\top (K + \\sigma^2 I)^{-1} K_*, \\qquad K(x, x') = \\sigma_f^2 e^{-\\frac{\\|x-x'\\|^2}{2\\ell^2}}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Predicting a delay from a few readings.</strong> Given the delay at 8:00, 8:10 and 8:45, you can predict 8:20 by leaning on nearby readings, and you are less sure at 9:30, far from any reading.",
    "examTrap": "Forgetting the noise variance regularization \u03c3\u00b2I when inverting K! Without \u03c3\u00b2I, near-collinear input points cause the kernel matrix K to become numerically singular, destabilizing the matrix inverse (K + \u03c3\u00b2I)^{-1}!",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Gaussian-process regression.</b> Place a Gaussian-process prior with kernel $K(x,x')=\\sigma_f^2e^{-\\|x-x'\\|^2/(2\\ell^2)}$ on $f$ and observe $y=f(X)+\\varepsilon$, $\\varepsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\sigma^2I)$. The posterior at $x_*$ is Gaussian with $\\mu_*=K_*^{\\top}(K+\\sigma^2I)^{-1}y$ and $\\Sigma_*=K_{**}-K_*^{\\top}(K+\\sigma^2I)^{-1}K_*$ (Rasmussen\u2013Williams 2).",
    "hint": "How do you fit a curve through noisy delay readings and say how sure you are?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$K(x,x')=\\sigma_f^2e^{-\\|x-x'\\|^2/2\\ell^2}$ = similarity.</strong> Inputs that are close have strongly related values; $\\ell$ sets how far the relation reaches.",
      "<strong>$\\mu_*=K_*^{\\top}(K+\\sigma^2I)^{-1}y$ = the predicted curve.</strong> A weighted combination of the observed values, weighted by similarity and corrected for noise.",
      "<strong>$\\Sigma_*=K_{**}-K_*^{\\top}(K+\\sigma^2I)^{-1}K_*$ = the uncertainty.</strong> The prior variance minus what the data explains; wide away from data."
    ],
