    "id": 32601,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "26_Martingales_and_Optional_Stopping_Theorem",
    "topicTitle": "26 \u00b7 Martingales & Optional Stopping",
    "cardNumber": "01",
    "category": "MARTINGALE FOUNDATIONS",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "Filtrations & The Three Martingale Definitions",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32601\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32601)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32601)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">day n</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">M\u2099</text><path d=\"M26.0,64.0 L36.3,56.0 L46.7,64.0 L57.0,56.0 L67.3,64.0 L77.7,72.0 L88.0,64.0 L98.3,56.0 L108.7,64.0 L119.0,56.0 L129.3,48.0 L139.7,56.0 L150.0,48.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">today n</text><text x=\"158\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">tomorrow: average</text><text x=\"158\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">of futures = M\u2099</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2131\u2099 = all you know by day n</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c[M\u2099\u208a\u2081 | \u2131\u2099] = M\u2099  \u21d2  \ud835\udd3c[M\u2099] = \ud835\udd3c[M\u2080]</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">a fair game: no drift up or down, on average</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A running tally M\u2099 and the information up to day n. A martingale is a fair game: the average of tomorrow's tally given the past is today's value M\u2099.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Filtration & Adapted Process:</strong> A filtration $\\mathbb{F} = \\{\\mathcal{F}_n\\}_{n \\ge 0}$ models increasing historical information over time: $\\mathcal{F}_0 \\subseteq \\mathcal{F}_1 \\subseteq \\dots \\subseteq \\mathcal{F}$. A process $M_n$ is adapted if its value is known by time $n$ ($M_n \\in \\mathcal{F}_n$).",
      "<strong>The Three Drift Regimes:</strong> An integrable adapted process $M_n$ satisfies: (1) **Martingale** (fair game) if $\\mathbb{E}[M_{n+1} \\mid \\mathcal{F}_n] = M_n$; (2) **Submartingale** (favorable game) if $\\mathbb{E}[M_{n+1} \\mid \\mathcal{F}_n] \\ge M_n$; (3) **Supermartingale** (unfavorable game) if $\\mathbb{E}[M_{n+1} \\mid \\mathcal{F}_n] \\le M_n$.",
      "<strong>Conservation of Expected Value:</strong> By the tower property of conditional expectation, every martingale preserves its unconditional expectation across all time: $\\mathbb{E}[M_n] = \\mathbb{E}[\\mathbb{E}[M_n \\mid \\mathcal{F}_0]] = \\mathbb{E}[M_0]$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mathbb{E}[M_{n+1} \\mid \\mathcal{F}_n] = M_n \\implies \\mathbb{E}[M_n] = \\mathbb{E}[M_0] \\quad \\forall n \\ge 0$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>A fair tally.</strong> Count minutes gained or lost against the schedule. If on average tomorrow's tally equals today's, whatever has happened, it is a martingale, and on average you end where you started.",
    "examTrap": "Confusing Submartingale and Supermartingale signs! A *sub*martingale has upward drift (E[X_{n+1}|F_n] >= X_n), while a *super*martingale has downward drift (E[X_{n+1}|F_n] <= X_n). Remember: sub = 'subterranean floor that pushes up'!",
    "stmtKind": "DEFINITION",
    "statement": "<b>Filtration and martingale.</b> A filtration $(\\mathcal F_n)$ is an increasing sequence of \u03c3-fields. $(M_n)$ is a martingale if $M_n$ is $\\mathcal F_n$-measurable, $\\mathbb E|M_n|\\lt \\infty$ and $\\mathbb E[M_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n]=M_n$. Then $\\mathbb E[M_n]=\\mathbb E[M_0]$ for all $n\\ge0$; with $\\ge$ or $\\le$ it is a sub- or supermartingale (Williams 10; Grimmett 12.1).",
    "hint": "What does it mean for a running tally to be a fair game given everything known so far?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\mathcal F_n$ = what you know by day $n$.</strong> The information grows with $n$; $M_n$ is known from it.",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[M_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n]=M_n$ = a fair game.</strong> Given the whole past, the expected tally tomorrow is today's tally: no drift up or down.",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[M_n]=\\mathbb E[M_0]$ = constant mean.</strong> Taking expectations of the martingale property gives a constant expected value for all $n$."
    ],
    "textbookImg": null,
    "textbookRef": null
