    "cardNumber": "04",
    "category": "OPTIONAL STOPPING",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-purple",
    "title": "The Optional Stopping Theorem (OST) & Conditions",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32604\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"12\" y=\"28\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"62.0\" y=\"51.0\" font-size=\"8.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c[M_\u03c4] = \ud835\udd3c[M\u2080]</text><rect x=\"124\" y=\"28\" width=\"102\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"175.0\" y=\"51.0\" font-size=\"8.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">if  \u03c4 \u2264 C</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">or  |M\u2099\u2227\u03c4| \u2264 K</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">or \ud835\udd3c\u03c4 &lt; \u221e and |\u0394M| \u2264 c</text><line x1=\"112.0\" y1=\"48.0\" x2=\"124.0\" y2=\"48.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32604)\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">when may you stop a fair game?</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">each condition rules out infinite waiting or unbounded losses</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">(doubling forever breaks it)</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The conclusion \ud835\udd3c[M_\u03c4] = \ud835\udd3c[M\u2080] holds under any one of the three conditions: bounded stopping time, bounded stopped tally, or finite mean wait with bounded steps.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Theorem Statement:</strong> Let $M_n$ be a martingale and $\\tau$ a stopping time. Then the expected stopped value equals the initial value, $\\mathbb{E}[M_\\tau] = \\mathbb{E}[M_0]$, if ANY ONE of the following sufficient conditions holds: (1) $\\tau$ is bounded almost surely ($\\tau \\le C$); (2) $\\tau \\lt  \\infty$ a.s. and the stopped process is bounded ($|M_{n \\wedge \\tau}| \\le K$); or (3) $\\mathbb{E}[\\tau] \\lt  \\infty$ and step increments are uniformly bounded ($|M_{n+1} - M_n| \\le c$).",
      "<strong>The Martingale Doubling Fallacy:</strong> In a fair coin game, bet $\\$1, \\$2, \\$4, \\dots$ until the first win at $\\tau$. At stopping, profit $M_\\tau = +1$ with probability 1! Yet $\\mathbb{E}[M_0] = 0$. OST fails here because $\\mathbb{E}[\\tau] = \\infty$ and wealth $M_n$ drops without bound ($-\\infty$).",
      "<strong>Dominated Convergence Backbone:</strong> The proof relies on taking the limit of the truncated martingale identity $\\mathbb{E}[M_{n \\wedge \\tau}] = \\mathbb{E}[M_0]$ as $n \\to \\infty$, which requires uniform integrability or dominated convergence to exchange expectation and limit."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mathbb{E}[M_\\tau] = \\mathbb{E}[M_0] \\quad \\text{if } \\tau \\le C \\quad \\text{or} \\quad |M_{n \\wedge \\tau}| \\le K \\quad \\text{or} \\quad (\\mathbb{E}[\\tau] \\lt  \\infty \\text{ and } |\\Delta M| \\le c)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>A doubling system fails.</strong> Betting double each time until the first win seems to guarantee a profit, but it needs unbounded losses on the way, so none of the conditions hold and the expectation is not preserved.",
    "examTrap": "Applying OST without checking regulatory conditions! In the classic doubling strategy, M_\u03c4 = 1 with probability 1, but E[M_0] = 0. Blindly applying OST produces 1 = 0! Always verify one of the three regulatory conditions!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Optional stopping theorem.</b> If $(M_n)$ is a martingale and $\\tau$ a stopping time, then $\\mathbb E[M_\\tau]=\\mathbb E[M_0]$ provided one of: (a) $\\tau\\le C$ a.s.; (b) $\\tau\\lt \\infty$ a.s. and $|M_{n\\wedge\\tau}|\\le K$; (c) $\\mathbb E[\\tau]\\lt \\infty$ and $|M_{n+1}-M_n|\\le c$ (Williams 10.10; Grimmett 12.5).",
    "hint": "When can you stop a fair game at a random time and still expect to leave with what you came with?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\tau\\le C$ = bounded time.</strong> You stop by a fixed deadline, so the stopped-martingale theorem applies at $n=C$.",
      "<strong>$|M_{n\\wedge\\tau}|\\le K$ = bounded tally.</strong> The tally never exceeds $K$, so passing to the limit $n\\to\\infty$ is safe (dominated convergence).",
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[\\tau]\\lt \\infty$ and $|\\Delta M|\\le c$ = finite wait, bounded steps.</strong> Then $|M_{n\\wedge\\tau}|\\le|M_0|+c\\tau$, which is integrable."
    ],
